│   ├── Used APIs  : KafkaProducer, send, flush, add_callback, add_errback
│   ├── Pitfalls   : confluent-kafka uses librdkafka (C library) requiring system dependencies; confluent-kafka Producer.poll() must be 
│   │   called periodically for callbacks to fire; the current callback-only pattern may need a poll loop; Different configuration property 
│   │   names (e.g., 'bootstrap.servers' vs 'bootstrap_servers'); confluent-kafka's delivery reports use a different callback signature 
│   │   (err, msg) vs kafka-python's (record_metadata)
│   ├── Constraints: requires_connector
│   └── The code uses kafka.KafkaProducer for synchronous message production with callback-based async handling. confluent-kafka provides a 
│       compatible Producer API with similar send/flush semantics and callback support, making it a viable drop-in replacement for the same 
│       synchronous producer role.
└── load testing framework
    ├── Candidates : locust, k6
    ├── Used APIs  : Locust, constant, environment.events.request_success.fire, environment.events.request_failure.fire
    ├── Pitfalls   : Fundamentally different architecture: Locust uses Python classes with event hooks; k6 uses JavaScript scripts with 
    │   built-in metrics; Migration requires rewriting the entire test structure (KafkaLocust class, KafkaClient, event firing) into k6 
    │   JavaScript; k6's Kafka support typically requires xk6-kafka extension or external tools; No direct API mapping — Locust's 
    │   request_success/failure events map to k6's built-in checks and metrics, not a 1:1 API
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── Both locust and k6 are load testing tools capable of simulating Kafka producer load. The code uses Locust's event system for request
        tracking and constant wait time. k6 can achieve equivalent load generation with its JavaScript API and Kafka extensions, though the 
        programming model differs (Python classes vs JS scripts).

